@* HelpSigningPage: help topic "Reviewers and signing" at "/help/reviewers-and-signing". Static text and screenshots. *@
@page "/help/reviewers-and-signing"

<HelpTopicFrame Slug="reviewers-and-signing">
    <section class="card">
        <ul>
            <li>A review has reviewer <strong>slots</strong>. Each slot has a primary reviewer and, if you like, an
                alternate; either of them can sign the slot. Only members of the project can be reviewers, and only a
                member can sign.</li>
            <li>Signing asks for your password again, to confirm it is you. If you have turned on
                <a href="/help/two-factor">two-factor sign-in</a>, it also asks for a current code from your
                authenticator app. If you sign in with an identity provider
                (the sign-in page offers a <strong>Sign in with ...</strong> button), you have no password here:
                press <strong>Confirm identity</strong> on the slot, sign in again at the provider when asked, and
                you come back to the review to press <strong>Sign</strong>. The confirmation is good for one
                signature and a few minutes.</li>
            <li>A slot can be signed only when every checklist check is answered, no check is answered Fail, and no
                thread is open.</li>
            <li>Any later change to the review, such as a new comment or a changed answer, makes the earlier signatures
                out of date, and the reviewer signs again. Before it does that, ReviewCenter shows a warning that names
                the affected slots and signers, and you choose <strong>Confirm change</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong>.
                The review is <strong>Approved</strong> when every slot is signed for its current state.</li>
            <li>An approved review is <strong>locked</strong>: nobody can add comments, change answers, change the
                files or the reviewers. A project admin or a system administrator can <strong>reopen</strong> it with
                a reason; this is recorded in the audit trail, the old signatures are kept as history, and the
                reviewers must sign again.</li>
            <li>When the project requires independent reviewers (the default), the person who created a review cannot
                be one of its reviewers and cannot sign it. Only the person who created the review in ReviewCenter is
                checked; the authors of the commits are not.</li>
            <li>While the project rule <strong>The person who reopened a review cannot sign it again</strong> is on
                (the default), the administrator who reopened a review cannot sign it again, and a reopen that would
                leave a slot without anyone who may sign is refused.</li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="help-figure">
            <img class="help-shot" src="/help/reviewers-and-signing-1.png" loading="lazy"
                 alt="The Reviewers card of a review showing a slot with a primary reviewer and the signing form that asks for a password." />
            <figcaption>A reviewer slot and its signing form.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="help-figure">
            <img class="help-shot" src="/help/reviewers-and-signing-2.png" loading="lazy"
                 alt="The Reviewers card after signing, with a green badge reading Signed by Ada Admin and the date." />
            <figcaption>The slot after it is signed.</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </section>
</HelpTopicFrame>
